From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhugengyu <zhugengyu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] sdio: (recv) Bound the receive retry loop that holds the
 host claim

sd_recv() retries a failed receive every 10 ms, and only leaves the loop
after ten failures when at least one read succeeded (rx_cnt != 0).  A
buffer or packet shortage before the first successful read therefore
loops forever - while the caller holds the host claim.  The transmit
thread that would free the buffer or packet the retry is waiting for
needs that claim, so the shortage can never clear.

Give up after ten attempts regardless of rx_cnt.  The request length is
left in place, so the next pump cycle re-reads the card's own request
length and retries from a released claim.

Signed-off-by: zhugengyu <zhugengyu@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 hal/rtl8188f/sdio/sdio_ops.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/hal/rtl8188f/sdio/sdio_ops.c
+++ b/hal/rtl8188f/sdio/sdio_ops.c
@@ -1549,8 +1549,19 @@
 					RTW_INFO("%s: recv fail!(time=%d)\n", __func__, alloc_fail_time);
 					phal->SdioRxFIFOSize = 0;
 				}
-				if (alloc_fail_time >= 10 && rx_cnt != 0)
+				/*
+				 * This loop runs with the host claim held, so an
+				 * unbounded retry here also blocks the transmit
+				 * thread that would free the very buffer or packet
+				 * the retry is waiting for.  Bound it: the next pump
+				 * cycle re-reads the card's request length and tries
+				 * again from a released claim.
+				 */
+				if (alloc_fail_time >= 10) {
+					RTW_INFO("%s: give up after %d tries, rx_cnt:%u ret:%u\n",
+					    __func__, alloc_fail_time, rx_cnt, ret);
 					break;
+				}
 			}
 			#endif
 		} else
